2026-07-26: Romain Nosenzo <romain@h3d.ai> (with Claude)
	Release 1.1.0.  Packaging work so the library can be distributed through
	vcpkg; no behaviour change for existing consumers (the default build is
	still a shared library with SIMD enabled).
	* CMakeLists.txt: linkage now follows BUILD_SHARED_LIBS instead of a
	hardcoded SHARED.  Static builds publish MINI_GMP_PLUS_STATIC as a PUBLIC
	usage requirement so the MINI_GMP_PLUS_API macros expand correctly in
	consumers as well, and POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE is enabled so the static
	archive can be linked into shared consumers.  VERSION/SOVERSION derive
	from PROJECT_VERSION instead of duplicated literals.
	* CMakeLists.txt: fixed an xsimd export leak visible only in static
	builds.  CMake records a static library's PRIVATE dependencies as
	transitive link requirements, so consumers received "-lxsimd" and failed
	to link.  xsimd is header-only and used by no installed header, so it is
	now wrapped in $<BUILD_INTERFACE:> and not exported.
	* CMakeLists.txt: install MiniMPF.hpp and mini-gmp-plus-config.hpp.
	MiniMPF was previously unusable from an installed copy -- neither it nor
	the config header it includes was installed.
	* CMakeLists.txt: raised cmake_minimum_required to 3.16 (3.5 is
	deprecated under CMake 3.31+ and removed in CMake 4) and declared
	MINI_GMP_PLUS_WITH_TESTS as a real option(); it was consumed but never
	declared, so it never appeared in the cache.
	* COPYING.GPL2, COPYING.GPL3: added.  mini-gmp grants "LGPL-3.0-or-later
	or GPL-2.0-or-later", but only the LGPL-3.0 text was shipped, and
	LGPL-3.0 is defined as a supplement to GPL-3.0.
	* vcpkg.json: repurposed as a development manifest for building this
	repository with the vcpkg toolchain.  The port manifest that packages
	mini-gmp-plus for consumers now lives under packaging/vcpkg/.
	* Removed the empty VCPKG_PREPARATION_SUMMARY.md and
	VCPKG_PORT_SUBMISSION.md stubs and rewrote VCPKG_INTEGRATION.md, which
	claimed all public headers were installed and that dynamic linking worked
	on every platform; neither was true.

2026-07-25: Romain Nosenzo <romain@h3d.ai> (with Claude)
	Small linear algebra: fused multiply-accumulate primitives, plus
	cheaper determinant algorithms in the geometry workloads.  Profiling
	the 2x2/3x3/4x4 determinant and dot-product kernels showed that the
	body of mpz_aorsmul_fast was ~50% of self time — more than all the
	limb arithmetic it performs — because every mpz_addmul/mpz_submul pays
	a full sign dispatch, magnitude comparison, normalization and
	reallocation.  The fix is to make each library call do more work.
	* mini-gmp.c, mini-gmp.h (mpz_mul_add_mul, mpz_mul_sub_mul): new
	public primitives computing r = a*b + c*d and r = a*b - c*d (a 2x2
	determinant).  Both products are formed in stack scratch and combined
	with a single sign resolution and normalization.  r may alias any
	operand.  Products larger than 32 limbs take a generic path.
	* mini-gmp.c, mini-gmp.h (mpz_dot_product): new public primitive
	computing r = sum u[i]*v[i].  Positive and negative terms go to two
	stack accumulators — one mpn_mul and one mpn_add per term, with no
	per-term sign comparison — reconciled by a single subtraction.  Much
	faster than an mpz_addmul chain once products exceed the inline limb
	buffer (-57% at 192-bit operands, where the chain starts allocating).
	* tests/geometry_workloads.hpp: determinant3 now uses cofactor
	expansion (5 library calls, was 13 for Sarrus); determinant4 uses
	Laplace expansion by complementary 2x2 minors (17 calls, was 56 via
	four full 3x3 determinants), which also reuses each minor across
	terms; determinant2 and dot_product are single fused calls.
	* tests/t-fused.c, tests/CMakeLists.txt: regression tests for the new
	primitives (random operands spanning the 32-limb cap, every sign
	combination, zero terms, exact cancellation, and r aliasing each
	operand).
	Geometry benchmark, Apple M2, Release+SIMD, interleaved best-of-4,
	checksums unchanged: det4 496 -> 275 ns/op (1.80x), det3 111 -> 91
	(1.22x), det2 24.2 -> 22.2 (1.09x), dot4 46.9 -> 44.0 (1.07x).
	The determinant gains grow with entry size: at 128-bit entries det4 is
	3.6x and det3 2.2x, since the old form's intermediates overflowed the
	5-limb inline buffer into the heap.

2026-07-19: Romain Nosenzo <romain@h3d.ai> (with Claude)
	Performance work on the small-operand hot paths, driven by
	benchmarks/benchmark_geometry.cpp (all results verified against the
	GMP-comparison test suite, an invariant fuzzer, and ASan/UBSan):
	* mini-gmp.c (mpz_rootrem): for square roots, seed the Newton
	iteration with an exactly-verified hardware-double sqrt of the top
	limbs (seed > sqrt(y) proven via integer fixup) instead of the
	single-bit seed 2^ceil(bits/2).  Cuts division-based iterations from
	~8 to 3 for 384-bit radicands: sqrt benchmark ~2.3x faster.
	* mini-gmp.c (mpz_gcd, mpn_gcd_22, mpn_gcd_bin,
	mpn_make_odd_inplace): the binary-GCD loop now runs on raw limbs in
	place (fused compare/subtract/shift, pointer swaps) instead of mpz
	calls, drops to a new register-based 2-limb mpn_gcd_22 base case
	(previously only 1-limb mpn_gcd_11), and skips the initial division
	when both operands have the same limb count: gcd benchmark ~2.6x
	faster.
	* mini-gmp.c (mpz_div_qr, mpn_div_qr, mpn_div_qr_1_preinv):
	truncating divisions with dividends up to 16 limbs use stack scratch
	instead of heap-allocated temporaries; the qp==NULL 1-limb path
	shifts on the fly and no longer allocates at all.  Removes every
	malloc/free pair from the sqrt Newton loop and the gcd reduction.
	* mini-gmp.c (mpn_mul): straight-line 128-bit kernels for 1x1..4x1
	limb products (the dominant multiply-accumulate shapes in
	determinant workloads): det3/det4 benchmarks ~5-7% faster.
	* mini-gmp.c (mpz_mul): the operand-overlap test is skipped on the
	1x1-limb fast path, which never needs a temporary.
	* mini-gmp.c, mini-gmp-simd.cpp: the scalar mpn primitives
	(copyi/copyd/zero/zero_p/cmp/add_n/sub_n/lshift/rshift) are always
	compiled in mini-gmp.c, even in SIMD builds, so callers can inline
	them; the SIMD TU keeps only the genuinely vectorized routines
	(popcount/hamdist/com/and/ior/xor).  Fixes the SIMD build being
	measurably slower than the non-SIMD build on scalar-heavy paths.
	* mini-gmp.c (MINI_GMP_NO_STACK_PROTECTOR): six hot functions with
	explicitly size-checked local buffers opt out of the stack
	protector (overridable by defining the macro).
	* CMakeLists.txt: new opt-in options MINI_GMP_NO_STACK_PROTECTOR
	(build the library with -fno-stack-protector, worth a further
	~5-10% on small-operand workloads) and MINI_GMP_ENABLE_IPO (LTO);
	-fno-semantic-interposition is no longer passed on Apple platforms
	where it is a no-op.

